{"cuisine/seychellois-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/seychellois-cuisine","n":"Seychellois cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Seychelles","intro":[["Seychellois cuisine is the cuisine of the Republic of Seychelles, an archipelago country consisting of 115 islands off the Indian Ocean coast of Africa. Fish plays a prominent part in the country's cuisine because of its location in the Indian Ocean. Seychellois cuisine has been influenced by African, British, French, Spanish, Indian and Chinese cuisines."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"seychellois-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seychellois_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Seychelles Creole Cuisine","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Arnold Chetty, 2022","note":"Seychellois Creole family recipes gathered over decades by a retired local economist.","url":"","suggested":true}]}],"cont":"African cuisine"},"cuisine/yoruba-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/yoruba-cuisine","n":"Yoruba cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Cuisine of a people","intro":[["Yoruba cuisine refers to the numerous and diverse foods of the Yoruba people of Yorubaland (Yoruba native regions of West Africa). Some notable Yoruba food includes Ọ̀fadà, Àsáró, Mọ́í Mọ́í, Ẹ̀gúsí soup, Àbùlà, Àkàrà, Ilá Alásèpọ̀, Boli, Ogi and Ẹ̀fọ́ rírò with Òkèlè. Yoruba cuisine has gained global acclaim outside of Africa with restaurant chains selling Yoruba food. Also on the global front are the fine dining Yoruba-inspired Michelin star restaurants like Akoko, Chisuru and Ikoyi in London that also serve Yoruba dishes. Yoruba food have also ranked among top best African food on TasteAtlas, including dishes like Ẹfọ riro, Ẹba, Moi moi, Jollof rice, Egusi, Asun, amongst others."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoruba_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Amala (food)","dish/amala-food"],["Draw soup","dish/draw-soup"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Amala","ingredient/amala"],["Yam","ingredient/yam"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Cayenne pepper","ingredient/cayenne-pepper"],["Crayfish","ingredient/crayfish"],["Ewedu","ingredient/ewedu"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Iru","ingredient/iru"],["Ogbono","ingredient/ogbono"],["Okra","ingredient/okra"],["Palm oil","ingredient/palm-oil"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"African cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/3a/Egusi_soup_with_pounded_yam_and_assorted_meats.jpg/500px-Egusi_soup_with_pounded_yam_and_assorted_meats.jpg","w":500,"h":490,"by":"Onyenachi64","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egusi_soup_with_pounded_yam_and_assorted_meats.jpg"}},"cuisine/culture-of-st-kitts-and-nevis-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/culture-of-st-kitts-and-nevis-cuisine","n":"St. Kitts and Nevis cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of St. Kitts and Nevis","intro":[["The  culture of St. Kitts and Nevis, two small Caribbean islands forming one country, has grown mainly out of the West African traditions of the slave population brought in during the colonial period.  France and British colonists both settled the islands, and for a period of time the British imported indentured Irish servants.  The native Caribs, skilled warriors, defended their lands by attacking the colonies.  But by 1782, the British had gained control of St. Kitts and Nevis, which they retained until granting the islands their independence in 1983.  British influence remains in the country's official language, English, while some islanders speak an English-based Creole.  The influence of the French, Irish, and Carib seems less pronounced."],["The people of St. Kitts and Nevis are devoutly religious.  Several historic Anglican churches remain on Nevis, and fifty percent of the country's population still practices the religion.  Most other people belong to another Christian denomination, though there are some Rastafarians and a few followers of the Baháʼí Faith.  An old Jewish cemetery on Nevis proves that there was once a Jewish population as well, but currently there is no active Jewish community in the country."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"culture-of-st-kitts-and-nevis-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_of_St._Kitts_and_Nevis#Cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/shan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/shan-cuisine","n":"Shan cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Thailand","intro":[],"src":null,"wiki":"Shan cuisine","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"shan-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Burma: Rivers of Flavor","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Naomi Duguid, 2012","note":"Burma-wide cookbook with substantial coverage of Shan State dishes, not Shan food alone.","url":"https://www.hachettebookgroup.com/titles/naomi-duguid/burma/9781579654139/?lens=artisan","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781579654139"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/sicilian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/sicilian-cuisine","n":"Sicilian cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Italy","intro":[["Sicilian cuisine is the style of cooking on the island of Sicily. It shows traces of all cultures that have existed on the island of Sicily over the last two millennia. Although its cuisine has much in common with Italian cuisine, Sicilian food also has Greek, Spanish, Jewish, Tunisian, and Arab influences."],["The Sicilian cook Mithaecus, born during 5th century BC, is credited with having brought knowledge of Sicilian gastronomy to Greece: his cookbook was the first in Greek, therefore he was the earliest cookbook author in any language whose name is known."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"sicilian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sicilian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":3,"rows":[{"name":"The Sicily Cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Cettina Vicenzino","note":"Traditional Sicilian island recipes by Cettina Vicenzino, published by DK.","url":"https://dk.com/en-us/products/9781465491107-the-sicily-cookbook","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781465491107"},{"name":"Sicily: From The Silver Spoon","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"The Silver Spoon Kitchen","note":"Phaidon's regional Sicilian recipe collection.","url":"https://www.phaidon.com/en-us/products/sicily-from-the-silver-spoon","suggested":true},{"name":"Recipes from Sicily","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Katie Caldesi, Giancarlo Caldesi","note":"Sicilian home recipes gathered by the London Italian restaurateurs.","url":"https://penguin.co.uk/books/466272/recipes-from-sicily-by-katie-caldesi-and-giancarlo-caldesi/9781784887520","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781784887520"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":12,"links":[["Busiate","dish/busiate"],["Cassatella di Agira","dish/cassatella-di-agira"],["Crocchè","dish/crocche"],["Iris (pastry)","dish/iris-pastry"],["Maccu","dish/maccu"],["Panella","dish/panella"],["Pani câ meusa","dish/pani-ca-meusa"],["Parmigiana","dish/parmigiana"],["Pasta alla Norma","dish/pasta-alla-norma"],["Pasta con le sarde","dish/pasta-con-le-sarde"],["Pasta mollicata","dish/pasta-mollicata"],["Stigghiola","dish/stigghiola"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Pasta","ingredient/pasta"],["Ricotta","ingredient/ricotta"],["Basil","ingredient/basil"],["Eggplant","ingredient/eggplant"],["Tomato sauce","ingredient/tomato-sauce"],["Breadcrumbs","ingredient/breadcrumbs"],["Durum","ingredient/durum"],["Chickpea","ingredient/chickpea"],["Brown sauce","ingredient/brown-sauce"],["Béchamel sauce","ingredient/bechamel-sauce"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Ham","ingredient/ham"],["Mashed potato","ingredient/mashed-potato"],["Shellfish","ingredient/shellfish"],["Chickpea flour","ingredient/chickpea-flour"],["Lung","ingredient/lung"],["Spleen","ingredient/spleen"],["Extra virgin olive oil","ingredient/extra-virgin-olive-oil"],["Mozzarella","ingredient/mozzarella"],["Parmesan","ingredient/parmesan"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/3f/Pasta_alla_Norma_%282563876877%29.jpg/500px-Pasta_alla_Norma_%282563876877%29.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Paoletta S.","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pasta_alla_Norma_(2563876877).jpg"}},"ingredient/pecorino-romano-pdo":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/pecorino-romano-pdo","n":"Pecorino Romano PDO","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Pecorino romano is a hard, salty Italian cheese made from sheep milk, often used for grating over pasta or other dishes. The name pecorino means 'ovine' or 'of sheep' in Italian; the name of the cheese, although protected, is a description rather than a brand: [formaggio] pecorino romano means 'sheep's [cheese] of Rome'."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-pecorino-romano-pdo"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pecorino_romano"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Lazian or Roman","cuisine/roman-cuisine",[["Cacio e pepe","dish/cacio-e-pepe"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Pasta","ingredient/pasta"],["Pecorino romano","ingredient/pecorino-romano"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Spaghetti","ingredient/spaghetti"],["Spaghetti alla chitarra","ingredient/spaghetti-alla-chitarra"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/ee/Pecorino_romano_cheese.jpg/500px-Pecorino_romano_cheese.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Jon Sullivan","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pecorino_romano_cheese.jpg"}},"ingredient/hot-dog":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/hot-dog","n":"Hot dog","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["A hot dog is a grilled, steamed, or boiled sausage served in the slit of a partially sliced bun. The term hot dog can also refer to the sausage itself. The sausage used is a wiener (Vienna sausage) or a frankfurter (Frankfurter Würstchen, also just called frank). The names of these sausages commonly refer to their assembled dish. Hot dog preparation and condiments vary worldwide. Common condiments include mustard, ketchup, relish, onions in tomato sauce, and cheese sauce. Other toppings include sauerkraut, jalapeños, chili, grated cheese, coleslaw, bacon and olives. Hot dog variants include the corn dog and pigs in a blanket."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-hot-dog"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hot_dog"}],"sections"